The '''1994 United States Senate election in Missouri''' was held November 8, 1994. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] U.S. Senator [[John Danforth]] decided to retire and not seek re-election.<ref>{{cite news|last=Chilcote|first=Gary|title=Danforth announcement is a jolt|newspaper=[[St. Joseph News-Press]]|date=February 2, 1993|location=|page=1B|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/st-joseph-news-press/190811588/|via=[[Newspapers.com]]|access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref> Former [[Governor of Missouri|Governor]] [[John Ashcroft]] won the Republican primary and faced Democratic Congressman [[Alan Wheat]] in the general election. Ashcroft defeated Wheat in a landslide, winning 60 percent of the vote to Wheat's 36 percent.

==Republican primary==
===Candidates===
* [[John Ashcroft]], former [[Governor of Missouri]]<ref>{{cite news|last=Edwards|first=Robert|title=Ashcroft jumps into Senate race|newspaper=[[Springfield News-Leader]]|date=February 7, 1994|location=|page=1B|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-springfield-news-leader/190811914/|via=[[Newspapers.com]]|access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref>
* Joyce Lea, perennial candidate<ref>{{cite news|last=Edwards|first=Robert|title=Ashcroft is name to beat in GOP|newspaper=[[Springfield News-Leader]]|date=July 25, 1994|location=|page=1A, 6A|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-springfield-news-leader/190812236/|via=[[Newspapers.com]]|access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref>
* Joseph A. Schwan, retired federal employee<ref name="rich variety">{{cite news|last=Mannies|first=Jo|title=Senate Races Features Rich Variety|newspaper=[[St. Louis Post-Dispatch]]|date=May 8, 1994|location=|page=4B|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/st-louis-post-dispatch/190811338/|via=[[Newspapers.com]]|access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref>
* Ronald G. Halstead, businessman<ref name="rich variety"/>
* Doug Jones, sporting goods store employee<ref name="rich variety"/>

